# Black-box delivery scenarios

This Playbook takes a project, a confirmed requirements JSON file, and the installed path of this Skill. Its sole deliverable is a reviewed set of black-box delivery acceptance scenarios. A runnable application, application code, devices, test accounts, credentials, third-party integrations, resource preflight, white-box scenarios, and execution evidence are not prerequisites. Never ask Boss for the location of an unimplemented app in this workflow. Never declare application delivery accepted here.

Fundamental REVIEW principle: A requirement's acceptance scenarios are sufficient only when passing their eventual real execution with the required evidence would support delivering that requirement to users within its agreed usage scope without an obvious defect. For each important operation mode, user state, boundary and transition, imagine a plausible incorrect implementation. If that implementation could still pass every linked scenario, the review must fail and identify the escaping fault. When unsure how to review a requirement, use this counterfactual question to decide what to inspect or add. Requirement-ID coverage and scenario count alone never establish sufficiency. Design review cannot itself prove the App works or that every possible bug is absent.

The ledger is `<project>/.delivery-acceptance/`, is Git-ignored, and follows `<skill>/references/data-model.md`. The deterministic stage checker is `python3 <skill>/scripts/playbook.py check <project> --stage black-box`. Never put real credentials in the ledger. This is already an active Playbook session: neither Captain nor any role may invoke `run_workflow.py`, `/black-box-scenarios`, or another Playbook from inside it. All role outcomes leave Git HEAD and Git-visible worktree unchanged.

Roles: Analyst creates and repairs scenarios and methods under the ignored ledger. Reviewer independently checks requirement coverage and scenario meaning. Captain reports the terminal result. Reviewer must not modify Analyst's ledger; only Analyst repairs it. The deterministic `script` actor independently reruns the stage checker after a positive Reviewer verdict. Only a zero exit may produce `scenariosReady`. A failed checker returns to Analyst for repair, at most three review rounds. No Tester role and no test execution in this workflow.

## Entry

Analyst verifies that the supplied requirements file contains a confirmed version, source and stable, unique IDs with nonempty text. If absent or ambiguous, return `requirementsGap` with exact `failureDetails`; do not invent or approve requirements. Otherwise run `python3 <skill>/scripts/playbook.py init <project> --requirements <requirements-file>` on first use, or `import-requirements` when the approved source changed. If an initialized ledger already has the same requirements snapshot, reuse it without deleting prior decisions. A changed approved requirements snapshot invalidates prior scenario completeness. Return `ready` after successful initialization or import.

## Design

Analyst maps every confirmed requirement to at least one black-box scenario. Derive relevant operation modes, user states, normal and exceptional behavior, boundaries and transitions from the confirmed requirements; include important combinations, not just the easiest happy path. A method must start from a reproducible state, perform the actual user action, and assert an observable result that would fail for an incorrect implementation. The scenario must be observable from the user or external system boundary and must not claim a particular implementation. Record in `.delivery-acceptance/scenarios.json` with `kind: black_box`, `title`, `requirement_ids`, `expected`, and `method_ids`. Record reusable AI-operated methods in `methods.json` with concrete `preconditions`, `actions`, `evidence_expected`, and `description`; these are plans for later execution, not evidence that execution occurred. Existing test-case references may be linked if actually present. Set `black_box_complete` only after assessing every confirmed ID. Do not set `white_box_complete` without reviewing implemented design/code. Preserve any existing white-box scenarios and resource decisions; a new requirements import already invalidates completeness. Analyst must not author or mark the independent `coverage_review.json` as passed. Return `done` when the draft is ready for independent review.

## Independent scenario review

Reviewer independently inspects the confirmed requirements, scenarios and methods. Apply the fundamental principle above to **every requirement**: enumerate its important operation modes and user states, including relevant normal, exceptional, boundary and transition behavior. For each important mode and state, propose a plausible incorrect implementation and determine which linked scenario would actually fail and what observable result would expose it. Challenge the operation-mode and state list itself; do not merely review the Analyst's selected cases. If a plausible important fault can escape, if a method cannot produce its claimed failure signal, or if a requirement is too ambiguous to know the intended outcome, return `gaps` with exact requirement IDs, missing modes/states and counterexamples. Do not treat one scenario per ID, an unchanged screen, or a vague assertion as sufficient evidence.

Reviewer alone writes `.delivery-acceptance/coverage_review.json` following `<skill>/references/data-model.md`: one record per requirement with `operation_modes`, `user_states`, `fault_probes` (mode, state, plausible_fault, linked scenario_ids, observable failure_signal), a reasoned verdict and `uncovered_paths`. Obtain the three scoped snapshot hashes with `python3 <skill>/scripts/playbook.py review-hashes <project>` after reading the current scenarios and methods. Mark the review `passed` only if every requirement is sufficient and no important path remains uncovered. Reviewer does not edit Analyst's scenarios or methods. Then run `python3 <skill>/scripts/playbook.py check <project> --stage black-box` and report its exact requirements, black-box-scenarios and coverage-review gate results. Return `passed` only when the substantive counterfactual review and all three direct checker gates pass; otherwise return `gaps` with exact `failureDetails`. Resource, white-box, execution and run-evidence gates are not prerequisites to this design review. Do not ask Boss to approve an ordinary scenario list.

After `passed`, the Playbook's `script` actor runs `python3 <skill>/scripts/playbook.py check <project> --stage black-box > <project>/.delivery-acceptance/black-box-gate.json`. A zero exit is required. A nonzero exit returns to Analyst for repair if review rounds remain. Analyst repairs only the named gaps in ignored ledger files, preserving confirmed requirement IDs and existing valid scenarios; Reviewer then repeats independent review. If material gaps remain after three rounds, finish `incomplete` with exact gaps. If requirements are unavailable, finish `incomplete` with the exact requirement gap.

## Completion

After the Reviewer and script gate pass, Captain reports `scenariosReady` with a nonempty `response` naming the scenario ledger path, number of black-box scenarios, requirement coverage, and the black-box gate report. Completion is read-only: Captain must not edit any file, including tracked documentation or ledger files, or run more design or checker work. Explicitly state that no App behavior was executed or accepted. If a gate fails after allowed repairs, report `incomplete` with exact failure details. A `scenariosReady` result is a successful design deliverable, not full application acceptance. Return a nonempty `response` in either terminal outcome.
